  "query_preview": "..\nToday and tomorrow: Windy conditions and scattered showers and \nthunderstorms will continue through the evening hours. The \nenvironment looks fairly unimpressive but still conducive for \nshowers and embedded thunderstorms with 100-300 J/kg of surface \nbased CAPE with weak to no capping inversion. The main risks from \nthese showers and storms will be brief outflow gusts up to 50 mph, \nisolated lightning, and pea size hail between 2-9 PM. Surface \npressure gradients have steady increased to 11.9 mb from \nPortland to Kalispell late this morning and into the early \nafternoon as high pressure noses into the WA/OR coast and recent\nwind gusts reflect that around 25-45 mph. These westerly winds \nwill continue through the evening and even overnight as the \nGEFS/ECMWF ensembles maintain at least a 11 mb gradient between \nPortland and Kalispell. Westerly wind gusts will slowly ease off\ninto Wednesday afternoon but stay breezy around 10-15 mph with \ngusts around 15-25 mph. Shower chances will decrease through the\nday Wednesday as the trough departs in MT. \n\nThursday through Tuesday: There is high confidence in a warming \ntrend starting Thursday and lasting through the weekend as a ridge \nbuilds off the west coast. The ridge axis will primarily will favor \nthe warmer temperatures on the west side of Washington and central \nWashington through Sunday. Getting into Monday and Tuesday next week \nis more uncertain as ensembles have a weak shortwave embedded in the \neast side of the ridge in Alberta and sliding south. A stronger wave \ncoming into our area would favor slightly cooler temperatures, \nparticularly north Idaho and far eastern Washington. Temperatures \nwill steady increase from the 70s and 80s Thursday to the 80s and \n90s by Sunday. Without disruption from this wave, there is potential \nfor temperatures Monday and Tuesday to be in the high 80s to high \n90s, although this period carries more uncertainty. DB",
